Gestalt principles
Gestalt principles are a set of rules that describe how humans perceive and organize visual information into meaningful patterns or groups. Some of the most common Gestalt principles are similarity, proximity, continuity, closure, and figure-ground. By applying these principles, you can create clear and coherent UX design that helps users understand the structure, hierarchy, and relationships of the elements on the screen. For example, you can use similarity to group related items by color, shape, or size; proximity to place items that belong together close to each other; continuity to create smooth and uninterrupted flows of information; closure to complete incomplete shapes or forms; and figure-ground to distinguish the foreground from the background.
